\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\i0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Specifies what an NSDecimalNumber will do when, in the course of applying 
\i method
\i0  to 
\i leftOperand
\i0  and 
\i rightOperand
\i0 , it encounters 
\i error. \

\fs14 \

\i0\fs28 There are four possible values for 
\i error
\i0 . The first three have to do with limits on NSDecimalNumber's ability to represent decimal numbers. An NSDecimalNumber can represent any number that can be expressed as 
\i mantissa
\b\i0 €x
\b0 €10
\f2\b\fs24 exponent
\f0\b0\i\fs28 , 
\i0 where 
\i mantissa
\i0  is a decimal integer up to 38 digits long, and 
\i exponent
\i0  is between -256 and 256. If these limits are exceeded, the NSDecimalNumber returns one of the following errors.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx1800\tx6300\tx6580\tx15140\fs28\fi-300\li1800\fc0\cf0 ·	NSCalculationLossOfPrecision. The number can't be represented in 38 significant digits.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	NSCalculationOverflow. The number is too large to represent.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	NSCalculationUnderflow. The  number is too small to represent.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 The last error is simpler:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx1800\tx6300\tx6580\tx15140\fs28\fi-300\li1800\fc0\cf0 ·	NSCalculationDivideByZero. The caller tried to divide by zero.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 In implementing 
\b exceptionDuringOperation:error:leftOperand:rightOperand
\b0 , you can handle each of these errors in several ways:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx1800\tx6300\tx6580\tx15140\fs28\fi-300\li1800\fc0\cf0 ·	Raise an exception. For an explantion of exceptions, see the NSException class description in the 
\i Foundation Framework Reference
\i0 .\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Return 
\b nil
\b0 . The calling 
\i method
\i0  will return its value as though no error had occurred. If 
\i error
\i0  is NSCalculationLossOfPrecision, 
\i method
\i0  will return an imprecise valueÐthat is, one constrained to 38 significant digits. If 
\i error
\i0  is NSCalculationUnderflow or NSCalculationOverflow, 
\i method
\i0  will return NSDecimalNumber's 
\b notANumber
\b0 . You shouldn't return 
\b nil
\b0  if 
\i error
\i0  is NSDivideByZero.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Correct the error and return a valid NSDecimalNumber. The calling method will use this as its own return value.\

\fs14 \

